-- Sesli rehberin İngilizce yazılı metni (audio_text_en). [1 second pause] işaretleri uygulamada gösterilmez.
-- Sütun yoksa oluşturulur. Güvenle yeniden çalıştırılabilir.
alter table public.heritage_places add column if not exists audio_text_en text;

update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$The sound of the kanun mingling with the murmur of water challenged the medicine of six centuries ago. [1 second pause] Built by Sultan Bayezid I in the 1390s, the Yıldırım Hospital is the first medical center of the Ottomans. The sound of water rising from the marble pool in its courtyard, together with music, brought healing to patients with mental illness. Surgeons in its 20 patient rooms entered medical history by obtaining court-approved consent forms from patients before operations. [1 second pause] It is a timeless monument where compassion took the form of stone.$t$ where id = 1;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Stone masses left unfinished as Timur's elephants trampled the city witnessed the revival of an empire. [1 second pause] The bathhouse, begun by Yıldırım Bayezid and completed by Çelebi Mehmed, takes its name from the shape of its basins. The beams of light filtering through the 93 elephant-eye glass panes in its dome, and the smoke channels beneath the floor, were remarkable feats of engineering. [1 second pause] Reborn from ruin as a cultural center, the bathhouse is the city's memory of resilience.$t$ where id = 2;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Laments sung after the martyrs turned into a proud fortress of compassion in Alacahırka. [1 second pause] Founded by Hacı Saffet Bey in 1916, the Dul Nisvân-ı Müslimîn took in the wives of martyrs and orphans. With its 34 rooms, bathhouse and bakery, it was a fortress of human dignity. The rent from the bakery below was paid as a 'headmistress salary' to a literate martyr's widow living at the institution. [1 second pause] It is the most graceful form of loyalty clothed in architecture.$t$ where id = 3;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$A black crown, a circular wooden floor, and the cry of 'hû' mingling with the sound of the ney... [1 second pause] Taking its name from the black turban of Sheikh Yakub Çelebi in the 16th century, the lodge is the mystical heart of Bursa. Its semahane, built in 1822, is covered with eight wooden columns and an ornamented dome. This floor, where dhikr echoed for centuries, became for a while after 1925 the 'Idman Yurdu', where young people trained. [1 second pause] It is the living soul of Bursa.$t$ where id = 4;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$The crackle of silk cocoons mingled with the bells of caravans in this vast courtyard. [1 second pause] Built in 1491 by Sultan Bayezid II through the architect Abdülûlâ, the Koza Han was the heart of the silk trade linking East and West. The pavilion masjid with a fountain in its courtyard is the elegant emblem of the building. Merchants arriving from Italy and Iran struck their silk bargains between these stone walls. [1 second pause] It is a monumental landmark woven into the texture of silk.$t$ where id = 5;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Horseshoes echoing beneath massive stone vaults announced the strong rhythm of trade. [1 second pause] Built in 1508 by Sultan Bayezid II through the architect Yakub Şah, the Pirinç Han provided income for his charity in Amasya. Rice and goods were stored in the cool vaults of the ground floor, while foreign merchants stayed in the rooms upstairs. [1 second pause] It is a timeless bulwark where the durability of stone meets the abundance of trade.$t$ where id = 6;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$A master builder's loyalty to his sovereign became a magnificent temple of trade in the heart of the city. [1 second pause] Before 1419, the architect Hacı İvaz Pasha founded the Geyve Han with his own wealth and donated it to Çelebi Sultan Mehmed and his Yeşil Complex. Its two-storey arcaded corridors hosted the hard bargaining of the cloth and yarn guilds. [1 second pause] It is a monument of fidelity in which a master's skill is crowned with the spirit of the foundation.$t$ where id = 7;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Seeing the glitter of huge mirrors beneath the dome of a bathhouse where hot steam once rose is one of history's sweetest surprises. [1 second pause] Built by Orhan Gazi in 1340, this double bathhouse is among the earliest examples of early Ottoman architecture. In the space converted into a bazaar in the 1960s, the mirrors hung by the shopkeepers gave it the name 'Aynalı Çarşı', the Mirrored Bazaar. [1 second pause] On walls once purified by water, the light of mirrors now shines.$t$ where id = 8;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Beneath twenty domes, the roar of water fit the sky on earth into a house of worship. [1 second pause] Built in 1400 by Yıldırım Bayezid through the architect Ali Neccâr, the Ulu Camii is the symbol of Bursa. The huge fountain inside brings freshness to the space. The 192 calligraphy panels on its walls, and the planetary orbits on its minbar, made in the kündekârî technique without a single nail, are an astonishing art. [1 second pause] It is a seal of infinity in the shade of twenty domes.$t$ where id = 9;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Imagine that historic moment when marble was reborn with tile, and the state with elegance... [1 second pause] Built in 1419 by Çelebi Sultan Mehmed through the architect Hacı İvaz Pasha, the Yeşil Mosque is the document of rebirth after the Interregnum. The marble carvings on its portal and the turquoise and navy underglaze tiles inside are like jewels. [1 second pause] A masterpiece that is an empire's cry of revival, it is the peak of turquoise.$t$ where id = 10;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$A single turquoise jewel glittering on the city's slopes engraved a state's destiny onto the sky. [1 second pause] Built in 1421 for Çelebi Sultan Mehmed by the architect Hacı İvaz Pasha, the tomb is the only monumental tomb whose exterior is entirely covered with tiles. The turquoise tiled sarcophagus and mihrab inside are the work of the tile master Mehmed el-Mecnûn. The sovereign who reunited the state sleeps in this turquoise armor. [1 second pause] It is the immortal soul of Bursa rising to the heavens.$t$ where id = 11;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$A two-storey architectural marvel with prayers rising below and springs of knowledge flowing above... [1 second pause] Built around 1385 by Sultan Murad I Hüdavendigâr, this building is unique in world architecture. The ground floor is a magnificent mosque, while the upper floor houses a madrasa of 16 cells. A design uniting worship and education in a single mass shows the vision of the early period. [1 second pause] It is the monument of a genius who broke the rules.$t$ where id = 12;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Over the roaring Gökdere, the sound of hammers and the clatter of cloth shears echoed. [1 second pause] Built in 1442 by the merchant Hoca Muslihittin through the architect Abdullah, the Irgandı is one of the four bridges with a bazaar in the world. The shops lined up on the single-arched stone structure are the signature of commercial genius over water. [1 second pause] The sounds of craft mingling with the river's noise have made it a legend living above the water.$t$ where id = 13;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Listen to a sorrowful and magnificent dynastic tale whispered by century-old cypresses... [1 second pause] Built in 1426 by Sultan Murad II, the Muradiye is the last sultanic complex in Bursa. With its mosque, madrasa and the 13 dynastic tombs in its garden, it is a vast park of memory. It is the eternal home of princes and sorrowful sultans. [1 second pause] This shaded garden, where sorrow and beauty lie side by side, is a farewell song.$t$ where id = 14;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$The gentle breeze behind the marble arcades invites you into the peace of the city's spiritual patron. [1 second pause] Founded in the early 15th century by Hundi Hatun in the name of her husband, the great mystic Emir Sultan, the complex is the spiritual refuge of Bursa. Its courtyard with marble arcades and a fountain displays the harmony of wood and stone. Renewed in 1804, the building refreshes hearts. [1 second pause] It is an island of peace as clear as water flowing from the marble fountain.$t$ where id = 15;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Witness how, while a state rose from its ashes, architecture turned into a feast of art... [1 second pause] Built in 1414–1419 by Çelebi Mehmed through the architect Hacı İvaz Pasha, the Yeşil Complex is a unified masterpiece with its mosque, tomb, madrasa and bathhouse. The bold use of the multicolored underglaze tile technique proclaims the might of the state. [1 second pause] This turquoise crown set on a natural hill is an eternal monument.$t$ where id = 16;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Light filtering from under wooden eaves illuminated the deep humility of a plain sultan. [1 second pause] Built by Sultan Murad II in 1426, the mosque is the T-plan, zaviye-type building at the heart of the complex. Its alternating stone and brick walls, geometric exterior ornament and wooden ceiling craftsmanship offer a deep elegance free of exaggeration. It reflects the dervish-sultan outlook. [1 second pause] It is the moment when stone is clothed in humility.$t$ where id = 17;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$The light of candles burning in stone cells turned into a centuries-old spring of knowledge illuminating minds. [1 second pause] Built by Sultan Murad II in 1426, the madrasa is the pride of classical Ottoman educational architecture. With its open courtyard, 16 student cells and tiled lecture-hall iwan, it dispensed learning. Today it serves as the Manuscripts Museum. [1 second pause] It is the home of the idea, where knowledge echoed in its stone cells.$t$ where id = 18;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Neither a sarcophagus nor an ornament... only an open dome and a sultan's body resting directly on the earth. [1 second pause] Built in 1451 by his son Mehmed the Conqueror, following Sultan Murad II's will, the tomb is the most modest sovereign's grave in the world. In keeping with the sultan's wish 'let the mercy of rain fall upon me', the center of the dome was left open and his body was buried in the earth. [1 second pause] The soil washed by rain is a monument to humility.$t$ where id = 19;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$The sorrowful shadow of a princely drama was covered by the glitter of the most magnificent tiles... [1 second pause] Built in 1479 by Mehmed the Conqueror for Prince Mustafa, and where Cem Sultan was also buried, the tomb is the most ornate building of the Muradiye. Its hexagonal turquoise and navy tiles and gold-gilded painted decoration offer extraordinary richness. The prince who found peace after exile lies here. [1 second pause] It is eternity in the light of turquoise tiles.$t$ where id = 20;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$A mother's prayer, blended with her grief for her child, turned into a graceful tomb among cut stones. [1 second pause] Built at the end of the 15th century by Şirin Hatun, wife of Bayezid II, the tomb is the hidden jewel of the Muradiye cemetery. Its square plan, cut-stone-and-brick courses and the painted decoration inside display the aesthetic world of the court ladies. [1 second pause] It is a pearl in which a mother's love and grace turned to stone.$t$ where id = 21;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$The aroma of soup boiling in huge cauldrons brought rich and poor to the same table for centuries. [1 second pause] Built in 1414–1419 by Çelebi Sultan Mehmed through the architect Hacı İvaz Pasha, the Yeşil Imaret is the kitchen of a civilization of compassion. This soup kitchen, where two hot meals were served every day, is the spirit of sharing that reached the capillaries of society. [1 second pause] It is a timeless monument, an inexhaustible hearth of generosity.$t$ where id = 22;
update public.heritage_places set audio_text_en = $t$Columns climbing from the open courtyard to the sky hosted the brightest minds of the Early Ottoman period. [1 second pause] Built in 1414–1424 by Çelebi Sultan Mehmed through the architect Hacı İvaz Pasha, the Sultaniye Madrasa is a three-iwan temple of learning that carried the Seljuk heritage into the Ottoman era. With its 13 cells and lecture hall, it is today the Museum of Turkish and Islamic Arts. [1 second pause] It is a bridge of knowledge stretching from the Seljuks to the Ottomans.$t$ where id = 23;

-- DİKKAT (Ulu Camii): Türkçe sesli rehber metni mimarı 'Hacı İvaz Paşa' diye veriyor, oysa eser kartındaki mimar Ali Neccâr.
-- İngilizce metin doğru yazıldı. Türkçe metni düzeltmek için aşağıdaki satırı açın; kayıtlı ses dosyası aynı hatayı
-- söylüyorsa metinle ses uyuşmaz, sesi de yeniden üretmek gerekir.
-- update public.heritage_places set audio_text = replace(audio_text, 'Mimar Hacı İvaz Paşa’ya', 'Mimar Ali Neccâr’a') where id = 9;
